          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Informally, a
            %strong diagram
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            consists of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            connected by some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi k
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  ^h\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^k\\
                  C&amp; \underset{g}{\to} &amp; D}
          %p by drawing some objects (or dots labeled by objects) connected by arrows labeled by morphisms.
          %p
            This terminology is often used when speaking about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
          %p
            There are two natural ways to give the notion of “diagram” a formal definition. One is to say that a diagram is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
          %p
            On the other hand, a more direct representation of what we draw on the page, when we “draw a diagram,” only involves labeling the vertices and edges of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+category"} free category
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#CategoryShapedDiagrams Diagrams shaped like categories
          %p
            We discuss here diagrams of the “shape of a small category”, as well as the concept of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cones"} cones
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocones"} cocones
            over these and
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limiting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimiting
            (co-)cones. There is a quick abstract functorial definition
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#CategoryShapedDiagramFunctorially"} functorial definition
          %p and there is a more long-winded but more explicit definition in terms of components
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#CategoryShapedDiagramInComponents"} component definition
              \.
          %h4#CategoryShapedDiagramFunctorially Functorial definition
          %p We state the concise functorial definition of diagrams of the shape of categories.
          #AbstractDefinition.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong (functorial definition)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℐ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{I}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            %p Then
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %em
                    diagram
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    of shape
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℐ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{I}
                    in
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
